00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:02.869 align:start position:0%
we have long been hearing about one  important tool to fight the spread of  the coronavirus  contact tracing allows authorities to  respond when there's a new case  isolating those who've been near the  infected person the process is being  aided by technology that most of us  carry  and it's already showing results in  europe chris livesay  has the story in the united states  coronavirus is spreading like wildfire  and melissa thomas is stomping out the  embers calling on behalf of the new  haven health department  her mission is to break the chain of  infection by making a list of  everyone who encountered a patient as  she told cbs news back in may  then urging anyone who was exposed to  get tested  but imagine you're in a crowded street  and let's say i have coronavirus  how could i possibly notify all of these  people  once my test results come back well the  idea is  with an app you wouldn't have to  contact tracing apps are already being  used here in italy  germany and around europe thanks to a  rare collaboration by both google and  apple  the tech is free for any country to use  to develop its own app  using your phone's bluetooth signal it  can detect other phones that have  also downloaded the app if two users  come close enough  the phone records the data then later if  one of them tests positive for  coronavirus  the other will be notified of course  that's if the other person  also has the app marcus bechdahl writes  about tech in germany  he like most germans was skeptical at  first  privacy is a very strong value  even before germany south korea and  singapore started using  exposure notification but far more  invasively  those countries link infections to your  identity whereas the technology  developed by google and apple  does not there's no government database  it's just  phones talking to phones but so far  the u.s isn't using it dr tom frieden  the former director of the cdc  says there's no federal requirement to  contact trace  in the u.s we've really lacked a clear  national strategy  frieden also cautions that apps cannot  replace traditional contact tracing  but the really ambitious idea requires  that  a large proportion of people opt into  that app  requires that the bluetooth signal works  where you're at and requires that you  don't have false positives and false  negatives  now uptake in italy has been slow only  about four million downloads here  so far but germany's app has been  downloaded by 20  of the country amid a pandemic with few  solutions in sight  it's a start for cbs this morning  saturday  chris livesay rome
 

